{"kind":"request","text":"codex: hugh's third layering finding, and I think he is right that the risk here is silence rather than error. He rates it Medium severity, small size, Medium risk.\n\nWHAT I VERIFIED. gitseq host/identity/resolve.go deliberately exposes two axes on Resolved, Vouching at line 44 and Verification at line 45, with the comment that they are already reduced along any path. Those exist so an application can say how strong an anchor has to be before it means something. Chess never asks. chess.go:595 seatAt gates on resolved.Anchored and chessScope alone, and a grep across every Go file in the chess repository returns no reference to Vouching or Verification anywhere. So chess treats the weakest anchored rung and the strongest as identical for the purpose of holding a seat.\n\nWHY THAT IS NOT NECESSARILY WRONG. This is a casual game. An anchor at any rung is still an endorsement someone signed, and demanding a witnessed provider anchor before a person can recover their own seat would make the recovery path useless for exactly the people who need it. I would not be surprised if every-anchor-counts is the correct policy, and I am not asking you to tighten it.\n\nWHY IT STILL NEEDS DOING. The policy is currently expressed as an absence. Nobody wrote every anchor counts; the code simply never looked at the axes, and that reads identically. So if a weaker rung is added to host later, chess gains a new class of seat-recovery authority silently, without a decision, without a test failing, and without anyone reviewing the consequence. The difference between a chosen policy and an unexamined default is invisible in the code today and becomes visible only when it is too late. Making the choice explicit costs almost nothing now and is the only thing that will make a future host change surface as a question rather than as behaviour.\n\nTHE SHAPE I AM ASKING FOR. Name the threshold, document it where the seat rule is read, and express it as a check that is doing work rather than as a gap. Test the boundary so the policy is pinned. That is deliberately compatible with deciding that nothing changes behaviourally: an every-anchor-counts policy that is written down, checked deliberately and pinned by a mutation-sensitive test is a complete answer to this request.\n\nWHAT WOULD MAKE ME REFUSE IT. A head that changes who can hold or recover a seat. That is a real policy change affecting live games and it needs its own request, its own reasoning and hugh's view, not a slipstream on a documentation lane.","body":{"conditions":"Not urgent; sequenced behind the display-names repair, the playability design and the binding-recovery CLI. Decide, state and test the identity strength chess requires before an anchored key carries seat authority. The decision is yours to make and may legitimately be that every resolved anchor counts; what is refused is leaving it unstated. Whatever you choose, it must be written where a reader looking for the seat rule finds it, not only in a commit message, and it must be expressed in code as a deliberate check rather than as the absence of one, so that a future rung cannot acquire authority by default. Add regression tests written from the desired behaviour that pin the threshold at the boundary: an anchor at the weakest rung chess intends to accept is accepted, and if any rung is intended to be refused, one is refused. Prove the tests are mutation-sensitive by weakening the threshold at its decision site and watching the named assertion fire; if the decision is that every anchor counts, the test must still fail when the Anchored or scope check is removed, so that the policy is pinned rather than merely described. Do not change which keys currently hold seats: this names and defends the existing threshold, and any actual change to who can recover a seat is a separate request with its own review. gofmt, build, vet and the full suite green at the exact head with -count=1; exact clean pushed head with per-path artifacts; independent review. Do not merge. Write no initiative or type tags; proposal 5409bc64 is unratified.","severity":"normal","to":"09c97907402e7747d051c0c599d9eb463c48d19825820a3e4c433cde2e60e2a8"}}